<speak>
<p>In the new coordinates, Z to the fourth equals one. Only the four powers one, Z, Z squared, and Z cubed are needed. Every term of our sign polynomial has the form h sub a b, times Z to the a, times P to the b. The first index runs from zero to three; the second from zero to u squared minus one.<break time="700ms"/></p>
<p>Group the terms according to their power of Z. Follow the coefficient and its power of P as they move together. The terms with no Z go into the first group. Those with Z, Z squared, and Z cubed go into the other three groups. We are showing representative terms; the omitted powers of P continue in exactly the same way.<break time="700ms"/></p>
<p>For each a, call the polynomial inside its group H sub a of P. It is the sum of h sub a b times P to the b, over all u squared coefficient positions. Keeping the common power of Z outside gives h of Z and P equals H zero of P, plus Z H one of P, plus Z squared H two of P, plus Z cubed H three of P.<break time="700ms"/></p>
<p>Why is this expression unique? The monomials Z to the a times P to the b form the integer group-ring basis. Every basis position already has its own sign coefficient. Re-expand one group: the very same coefficients are still there. We have regrouped terms, without adding different coefficients together. Each H sub a therefore still has u squared coefficients, all plus or minus one. No norm identity was needed for this regrouping.<break time="700ms"/></p>
<p>In the guiding order thirty-six case, u is three, so each H sub a has nine coefficient positions. Keep these entries symbolic; this is not a constructed Hadamard row. Choose a single power, say P to the fourth. The four signs h zero four, h one four, h two four, and h three four line up in one column. These are the signs that will combine when we evaluate Z.<break time="700ms"/></p>
<p>We now have the four-polynomial expression ready for two independent checks. Substituting fourth roots for Z will preserve the norm identity. Checking sums and differences of the four signs will explain why division by two keeps the coefficients integral. Evaluation and parity will then come together in the normalization step.<break time="900ms"/></p>
</speak>
